[center][color=#888888][i]Disclaimer: this is AI slop, written by Claude Code and Codex. GPL-3.0, source public, judge it by whether it runs.[/i][/color][/center]

[line]

[center][size=6][b]Skyrim Upscaler -- DLSS 4 / DLSS 5 Neural Rendering / FSR[/b][/size]
[size=3]DLSS, DLAA, FSR, frame generation from either vendor, Ray Reconstruction and NVIDIA's Neural Rendering, with ENB.[/size][/center]

[line]

[center][size=5][color=#ff4444][b]DO NOT RUN THIS WITH COMMUNITY SHADERS[/b][/color][/size][/center]

[b]Not "it might conflict" -- it will not work.[/b] Community Shaders replaces the same part of Skyrim's renderer this plugin hooks into, and it already has its own upscaling built in. Two mods cannot own the same render pipeline.

[b]Install one or the other. Never both.[/b] If both are present, uninstall one before reporting anything.

[b]ENB is fine, and so is ReShade.[/b] Both draw into the same buffer this mod hands the game, and their output reaches the screen through it. Your ENB preset's tonemapping, colour grading and effects all still apply to the upscaled image.

ENB is the one that has been tested properly. ReShade works through the same mechanism but has not been run here -- if something is off with it, say so and include the log. One known wrinkle: ReShade effects that hunt for the game's depth buffer can be thrown by the quality modes, which change the resolution the scene renders at. Try [b]Native AA[/b] before concluding the two cannot coexist.

[line]

[size=5][b]What this does[/b][/size]

Three separate features. All of them are optional and you can run any combination.

[list]
[*][b]Upscaling -- DLSS, DLAA and FSR.[/b] Skyrim renders at a lower resolution and the upscaler rebuilds a full-resolution image. You get frames back. At [b]Native AA[/b] it does not lower the resolution at all and instead gives you far better anti-aliasing than Skyrim's own TAA.
[*][b]Frame Generation -- NVIDIA DLSS-G or AMD FSR.[/b] The GPU creates extra frames between the real ones. Pick the backend in the menu, or leave it on Auto and it uses DLSS-G where the card supports it and FSR where it does not. On RTX 40 cards the driver's 2x cap on DLSS-G can be lifted up to 6x.
[*][b]DLSS 5 Neural Rendering.[/b] NVIDIA's neural model reworks the finished image -- materials, lighting and skin stop looking flat. This is the newest feature and the hardest to get running.
[/list]

Everything is configured in game. No ini editing required.

[line]

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Skyrim Special Edition [b]1.5.97[/b] or Anniversary Edition [b]1.6.x[/b] -- VR and GOG are not supported
[*][url=https://skse.silverlock.org/]SKSE64[/url]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/120352]SKSE Menu Framework[/url]
[*]An NVIDIA RTX card for the DLSS features. FSR runs on anything.
[*][b]Community Shaders must not be installed[/b]
[/list]

[b]FSR upscaling[/b] -- any reasonably modern GPU
[b]FSR frame generation[/b] -- any reasonably modern GPU, including AMD and Intel
[b]DLSS / DLAA / Ray Reconstruction[/b] -- NVIDIA RTX
[b]DLSS-G frame generation[/b] -- NVIDIA RTX 40 or 50
[b]Neural Rendering[/b] -- NVIDIA RTX

[b]Every runtime is included in the download.[/b] Nothing else to find, nothing else to place.

[line]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install with your mod manager, or drop the [font=Courier New]SKSE[/font] folder into [font=Courier New]Data[/font].
[*]Launch, press [b]F1[/b] to open the Mod Control Panel, and pick [b]SkyrimUpscaler[/b].
[/list]

That is all of it. The NVIDIA and AMD runtimes are in the archive and go where they need to go on their own.

Every one of them is reported as [font=Courier New]found[/font] or [font=Courier New]missing[/font] in the log at startup, so if something did not survive your mod manager it says so plainly. A feature whose DLL is missing simply stays unavailable in the menu -- nothing breaks.

[line]

[size=5][b]About Neural Rendering[/b][/size]

The [font=Courier New]nvngx_dlssnr.dll[/font] NVIDIA ships only contains code for RTX 50-series cards. On a 20, 30 or 40 series card it loads and then refuses to run -- which is why this feature does not simply work everywhere.

The build in this download is the community-patched one, and it runs on Ada and earlier. That work is not mine. It comes from the [url=https://discord.com/invite/renodx][b]RenoDX[/b][/url] community, and [b]Neural Rendering in this mod would not exist without them.[/b] If this is the feature you came for, go and say thank you over there.

[line]

[size=5][b]About Frame Generation[/b][/size]

Read this before turning it on.

[list]
[*]It [b]doubles the number on your frame counter[/b] and makes motion look smoother.
[*]It does [b]not[/b] make the game feel more responsive. Input latency stays roughly the same or gets slightly worse.
[*]It needs a decent real frame rate to start with. Enabling it at 25 fps will not feel good.
[/list]

[b]Two backends.[/b] [b]Frame Generation Backend[/b] in the menu chooses between NVIDIA's DLSS-G and AMD's FSR. Auto takes DLSS-G where the card and runtime support it and FSR everywhere else, which is what puts frame generation within reach on hardware that is not an RTX 40 or 50.

[b]Changing the backend needs a restart[/b], and this is not laziness: the two generators work through different swapchains -- DLSS-G through Streamline's and FSR through FidelityFX's own -- and the game is handed exactly one, chosen before any of these settings can be changed. The menu will say so too.

[color=#ffaa00][b]Turn the Steam overlay off for Skyrim before using it.[/b][/color] Steam -> Skyrim -> Properties -> General -> In-Game Overlay. The overlay crashes when frame generation presents from its own thread. The mod refuses to run frame generation while it sees the overlay; there is a setting to override that, and if you do, a crash is on you.

[b]Honest warning:[/b] during development frame generation hung the GPU more than once, and produced a [font=Courier New]DPC_WATCHDOG_VIOLATION[/font] bugcheck on one occasion. That class of failure is fixed and it has since run for tens of thousands of frames without incident, but it is off by default for a reason. A watchdog latches it off for the session if a Present call blocks.

[line]

[size=5][b]Defaults[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Frame Generation -- [b]off[/b]
[*]Neural Rendering -- [b]off[/b]
[*]Upscaling -- [b]Native AA[/b]
[/list]

[b]Native AA does not raise your frame rate.[/b] It renders at full resolution and runs the upscaler on top, so it costs a few frames. You are paying for anti-aliasing, not for speed. If you want frames, pick Quality, Balanced or Performance.

[line]

[size=5][b]Status[/b][/size]

[b]Experimental.[/b] Every feature listed here has been seen working in game, on exactly one configuration: an RTX 4070 Ti, AE 1.6.1170, ENB, 1920x1080. No other hardware, runtime version or resolution has been tested by the author.

Known things worth saying out loud:

[list]
[*]Skyrim has no G-buffer, so Ray Reconstruction's normals are reconstructed from depth and its material inputs are constants. It works, but it is not being fed what the model was designed for.
[*][b]FSR frame generation is the newest part of this and the least tested.[/b] DLSS-G has thousands of frames behind it on the author's machine; the FSR path has almost none, on a machine where DLSS-G is what Auto picks. Treat it as the experimental corner of an already experimental mod.
[*]Frame Generation and the Steam overlay do not coexist. This applies to both backends -- the fault is in the overlay's present hook, not in either generator.
[*]Only Steam SE 1.5.97 and AE 1.6.x. VR and GOG are out of scope and will not be added.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Something is wrong[/b][/size]

The log is at:
[font=Courier New]Documents/My Games/Skyrim Special Edition/SKSE/SkyrimUpscaler.log[/font]

It is rewritten every launch and records what loaded, what did not, and why. [b]Attach it to any bug report[/b] even if it means nothing to you -- it usually means something to whoever reads it.

Quick checks before reporting:
[list=1]
[*]Turn Frame Generation off and try again. It is the single most likely cause of a freeze.
[*]Confirm Community Shaders is not installed.
[*]Check the log for a runtime DLL reported as [font=Courier New]missing[/font] -- if one is, the install did not land intact.
[/list]

[b]The screen went strange after I pressed a key?[/b] Press [b]F10[/b] a few more times. F10 cycles diagnostic views used during development, and some of them hide the entire interface. Keep pressing until it looks normal. Set [font=Courier New]UICompositeDebugKey = 0[/font] in the ini to disable the key entirely.

[line]

[size=5][b]Credits[/b][/size]

This mod is a port. Almost none of the hard problems it solves were solved here first.

[list]
[*][url=https://github.com/doodlum/fo4test][b]doodlum[/b][/url] -- where this lineage begins. Also the author of [b]Community Shaders[/b], which this page tells you not to run alongside this mod. That is a fact about two things interposing on one render pipeline and nothing more; it takes nothing away from the fact that this project stands on their work.
[*][url=https://github.com/jarari/fo4test][b]jarari[/b][/url] -- the Fallout 4 upscaler this is a port of. The D3D12 proxy swapchain, the present path, the Streamline and FidelityFX wrappers and the Neural Rendering backend all come through their fork. Their [font=Courier New]test/dlss-nr[/font] branch reconstructed the DLSS-NR interface NVIDIA has never published.
[*][url=https://discord.com/invite/renodx][b]RenoDX[/b][/url] and its community -- for the runtime DLLs that make DLSS 5 Neural Rendering possible here at all, and for getting the feature running on hardware NVIDIA had not enabled it for.
[*][url=https://github.com/dashdogy/RTX40MFG-Unlock][b]Michael Robles (dashdogy)[/b][/url] -- RTX40MFG-Unlock, which lifts the 2x frame generation cap on Ada.
[*][url=https://github.com/PureDark/Skyrim-Upscaler][b]PureDark[/b][/url] -- the public 2022 repository that identified the engine hook points everyone since has used.
[*][b]Boris Vorontsov[/b] -- ENBSeries. The present path in this mod is shaped almost entirely by the requirement to coexist with it.
[*][b]ianpatt, behippo, purple lunchbox[/b] -- SKSE64.
